1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:03,645
A rainbow looks like it's painted on the sky.

2
00:00:04,095 --> 00:00:07,105
But try walking toward one, and it backs away.

3
00:00:07,555 --> 00:00:10,007
Move to the side, and it moves with you.

4
00:00:10,457 --> 00:00:13,720
That's because a rainbow isn't a thing in the sky.

5
00:00:13,720 --> 00:00:14,633
It's an angle.

6
00:00:15,083 --> 00:00:19,015
And that angle is always the same: 42°.

7
00:00:19,015 --> 00:00:19,831
Here's why.

8
00:00:21,631 --> 00:00:23,596
Start with a single raindrop,

9
00:00:23,596 --> 00:00:25,561
and a single ray of sunlight.

10
00:00:26,011 --> 00:00:29,391
A small raindrop is not shaped like a teardrop.

11
00:00:29,391 --> 00:00:33,130
It's very nearly a perfect sphere, and that matters,

12
00:00:33,130 --> 00:00:36,797
because a sphere treats every ray by the same rule.

13
00:00:37,247 --> 00:00:40,281
When the ray hits the surface, it goes in,

14
00:00:40,281 --> 00:00:41,220
and it bends,

15
00:00:41,220 --> 00:00:45,192
because light travels more slowly in water than in air.

16
00:00:45,642 --> 00:00:48,523
It crosses the drop, and at the back surface,

17
00:00:48,523 --> 00:00:49,804
part of it reflects.

18
00:00:50,254 --> 00:00:53,874
Then it crosses the drop again and leaves through the front,

19
00:00:53,874 --> 00:00:55,202
bending a second time.

20
00:00:55,652 --> 00:00:58,867
So the drop sends that light back toward the side

21
00:00:58,867 --> 00:00:59,785
the sun is on.

22
00:01:01,585 --> 00:01:05,481
How far the light gets turned depends on where it hits the drop.

23
00:01:05,931 --> 00:01:08,981
A ray through the dead centre bounces straight back.

24
00:01:09,431 --> 00:01:11,012
Move the ray up a little,

25
00:01:11,012 --> 00:01:13,226
and it comes back at a small angle.

26
00:01:13,676 --> 00:01:16,117
Move it higher, and the angle grows.

27
00:01:16,567 --> 00:01:18,636
But watch what happens near the top.

28
00:01:19,086 --> 00:01:20,801
The angle stops growing.

29
00:01:20,801 --> 00:01:24,232
It reaches a maximum of about 42°,

30
00:01:24,232 --> 00:01:26,734
and then it starts to shrink again.

31
00:01:28,534 --> 00:01:30,911
That turning point is the whole secret.

32
00:01:31,361 --> 00:01:33,979
Look at the curve. On the steep part,

33
00:01:33,979 --> 00:01:37,234
a small change in where the ray enters makes a

34
00:01:37,234 --> 00:01:39,357
big change in where it leaves,

35
00:01:39,357 --> 00:01:42,612
so that light gets smeared across many angles.

36
00:01:43,062 --> 00:01:45,851
But at the top, the curve is flat. There,

37
00:01:45,851 --> 00:01:49,525
a whole band of rays shares almost the same direction.

38
00:01:49,975 --> 00:01:52,948
Sunlight doesn't hit a drop in one place.

39
00:01:52,948 --> 00:01:55,196
It covers the whole face of it.

40
00:01:55,196 --> 00:01:57,516
So send in all the rays at once.

41
00:01:57,966 --> 00:02:00,874
Everywhere else, the light is spread thin.

42
00:02:00,874 --> 00:02:02,743
But near 42°,

43
00:02:02,743 --> 00:02:05,236
the rays pile up into a bright beam.

44
00:02:05,686 --> 00:02:08,627
Every raindrop in the sky is doing this,

45
00:02:08,627 --> 00:02:13,186
sending out a cone of concentrated light at 42°.

46
00:02:14,986 --> 00:02:16,752
So where do the colours come from?

47
00:02:17,202 --> 00:02:19,790
White sunlight is a mix of every colour,

48
00:02:19,790 --> 00:02:23,024
and water bends blue light slightly more than red.

49
00:02:23,474 --> 00:02:25,643
That shifts the turning point.

50
00:02:25,643 --> 00:02:29,331
Red piles up at about 42.5°.

51
00:02:29,331 --> 00:02:31,789
Violet, at about 40.5°.

52
00:02:32,239 --> 00:02:35,195
Every colour in between gets its own angle.

53
00:02:35,645 --> 00:02:38,127
So one drop doesn't send you a rainbow.

54
00:02:38,127 --> 00:02:41,883
It sends each colour off in a slightly different direction.

55
00:02:43,683 --> 00:02:45,733
Now put yourself in the picture.

56
00:02:45,733 --> 00:02:47,142
The sun is behind you,

57
00:02:47,142 --> 00:02:49,448
and rain is falling in front of you.

58
00:02:49,898 --> 00:02:52,436
Which drops send their red light into your eye?

59
00:02:52,886 --> 00:02:56,786
Only the ones sitting 42° away from the point

60
00:02:56,786 --> 00:02:58,504
directly opposite the sun.

61
00:02:58,504 --> 00:03:01,941
That's the spot where the shadow of your head falls.

62
00:03:02,391 --> 00:03:03,822
The drops keep falling,

63
00:03:03,822 --> 00:03:06,496
but there is always one in the right place,

64
00:03:06,496 --> 00:03:07,989
so the colour stays put.

65
00:03:08,439 --> 00:03:10,339
Seen from where you stand,

66
00:03:10,339 --> 00:03:14,724
all the drops at that angle form a circle around that point.

67
00:03:14,724 --> 00:03:16,770
That circle is the red band.

68
00:03:17,220 --> 00:03:18,564
Slightly inside it,

69
00:03:18,564 --> 00:03:21,535
a different set of drops sends you violet,

70
00:03:21,535 --> 00:03:24,223
and the other colours fall in between.

71
00:03:26,023 --> 00:03:29,610
This is why you can never reach the end of a rainbow.

72
00:03:29,610 --> 00:03:31,369
It isn't located anywhere.

73
00:03:31,818 --> 00:03:32,632
Take a step,

74
00:03:32,632 --> 00:03:37,108
and a different set of drops is now at 42° from you.

75
00:03:37,108 --> 00:03:38,668
The bow moves with you.

76
00:03:39,118 --> 00:03:42,727
The person standing next to you sees a different rainbow,

77
00:03:42,727 --> 00:03:44,247
made by different drops.

78
00:03:44,697 --> 00:03:49,656
It's also why rainbows show up in the morning and the late afternoon.

79
00:03:49,656 --> 00:03:50,950
As the sun climbs,

80
00:03:50,950 --> 00:03:55,047
the centre of the circle sinks further below the horizon.

81
00:03:55,497 --> 00:03:58,750
Once the sun is higher than 42°,

82
00:03:58,750 --> 00:04:01,226
the whole bow is below the horizon,

83
00:04:01,226 --> 00:04:03,206
and there is nothing to see.

84
00:04:03,656 --> 00:04:06,829
And from an aeroplane, with no ground in the way,

85
00:04:06,829 --> 00:04:08,642
you can see the full circle.

86
00:04:10,443 --> 00:04:14,254
Sometimes there's a second, fainter bow outside the first.

87
00:04:14,704 --> 00:04:18,311
That's light that bounced twice inside the drop before leaving.

88
00:04:18,761 --> 00:04:21,574
Two bounces give a different turning point,

89
00:04:21,574 --> 00:04:23,536
about ten degrees further out,

90
00:04:23,536 --> 00:04:26,087
and they flip the order of the colours.

91
00:04:26,537 --> 00:04:29,690
Between the two bows, the sky looks darker.

92
00:04:29,690 --> 00:04:32,696
No drop sends light back at those angles.

93
00:04:32,696 --> 00:04:34,822
It's called Alexander's band.

94
00:04:35,272 --> 00:04:38,582
And inside the main bow, the sky is brighter,

95
00:04:38,582 --> 00:04:42,553
lit by all the light that came back at smaller angles.

96
00:04:44,352 --> 00:04:46,566
So a rainbow is not an object.

97
00:04:47,016 --> 00:04:50,271
It's what you get when millions of tiny spheres each

98
00:04:50,271 --> 00:04:52,586
fold sunlight back at the same angle,

99
00:04:52,586 --> 00:04:56,404
and you happen to be standing in the right place to catch it.

100
00:04:56,854 --> 00:04:58,764
Next time you see one, check.

101
00:04:58,764 --> 00:05:01,135
The sun will be directly behind you,

102
00:05:01,135 --> 00:05:03,770
and the centre of the bow will be at the

103
00:05:03,770 --> 00:05:05,351
shadow of your own head.

104
00:05:05,801 --> 00:05:07,242
That's why it does that.
